1
00:00:00,222 --> 00:00:02,137
Here's that spanner on a stuck nut.

2
00:00:02,862 --> 00:00:05,664
Its handle is twenty-five centimetres long, and each

3
00:00:05,745 --> 00:00:07,725
push on the end is eighty newtons.

4
00:00:08,587 --> 00:00:10,228
One push is at right angles to the

5
00:00:10,269 --> 00:00:10,568
handle,

6
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:13,271
one is at thirty degrees to it, and

7
00:00:13,412 --> 00:00:15,834
one points straight along the handle, towards the

8
00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:16,054
nut.

9
00:00:16,658 --> 00:00:18,145
Same force, same spot.

10
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:19,194
Rank them.

11
00:00:19,819 --> 00:00:21,200
Which turns the nut best,

12
00:00:21,842 --> 00:00:23,202
and does any of them do nothing at

13
00:00:23,303 --> 00:00:23,442
all?

14
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:30,060
Here's how strongly each push tries to turn

15
00:00:30,101 --> 00:00:32,064
the nut, shown by a curved arrow.

16
00:00:32,729 --> 00:00:35,122
The right-angle push has the biggest turning effect.

17
00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:38,220
The thirty degree push has one only half

18
00:00:38,281 --> 00:00:38,622
as big.

19
00:00:39,407 --> 00:00:41,631
And the push along the handle just shoves

20
00:00:41,712 --> 00:00:43,475
on the nut, with no turning effect at

21
00:00:43,556 --> 00:00:43,675
all.

22
00:00:44,698 --> 00:00:46,482
The turning effect of a force about a

23
00:00:46,563 --> 00:00:48,147
pivot is called torque.

24
00:00:48,872 --> 00:00:49,863
Torque isn't a force.

25
00:00:50,329 --> 00:00:52,397
It's what a force does to rotation.

26
00:00:53,181 --> 00:00:55,470
So why does the same force give more

27
00:00:55,551 --> 00:00:57,158
torque at some angles than others?

28
00:00:58,170 --> 00:01:00,260
Start with the simplest case, the push at

29
00:01:00,402 --> 00:01:00,944
right angles.

30
00:01:01,728 --> 00:01:03,932
A longer spanner makes a stuck nut easier,

31
00:01:04,394 --> 00:01:06,317
and a door pushed near its hinge barely

32
00:01:06,358 --> 00:01:06,677
moves.

33
00:01:07,361 --> 00:01:08,966
Distance from the pivot matters.

34
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:11,594
Push halfway along the handle, and you get

35
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:13,078
only half the turning effect.

36
00:01:13,981 --> 00:01:15,644
For a force at right angles to the

37
00:01:15,685 --> 00:01:17,808
line from the pivot, the torque is the

38
00:01:17,909 --> 00:01:19,311
distance times the force.

39
00:01:19,954 --> 00:01:21,367
Tau equals r F.

40
00:01:22,194 --> 00:01:25,159
Here that's zero point two five metres times

41
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:26,000
eighty newtons,

42
00:01:26,582 --> 00:01:27,803
twenty newton metres.

43
00:01:28,607 --> 00:01:30,421
Torque is measured in newton metres.

44
00:01:31,006 --> 00:01:32,832
It isn't energy, so we never write it

45
00:01:32,933 --> 00:01:33,454
in joules.

46
00:01:34,241 --> 00:01:36,089
Now the push at thirty degrees.

47
00:01:36,734 --> 00:01:38,208
Split it into two components.

48
00:01:38,754 --> 00:01:40,149
One runs along the handle.

49
00:01:40,615 --> 00:01:42,265
It just pulls on the nut through its

50
00:01:42,285 --> 00:01:43,613
centre, so it can't turn it.

51
00:01:44,216 --> 00:01:46,265
The other is at right angles to the

52
00:01:46,306 --> 00:01:48,676
handle, and only that part does the turning.

53
00:01:49,339 --> 00:01:52,466
It's F sin theta, where theta is the

54
00:01:52,647 --> 00:01:54,370
angle between the handle and the force.

55
00:01:55,354 --> 00:01:57,658
Eighty times sin thirty is forty newtons,

56
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:00,423
so the torque is zero point two five

57
00:02:00,484 --> 00:02:01,144
times forty,

58
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:02,747
ten newton metres.

59
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:05,557
Half the force at right angles, so half

60
00:02:05,578 --> 00:02:05,939
the torque.

61
00:02:06,684 --> 00:02:07,856
There's a second way to see it.

62
00:02:08,442 --> 00:02:10,834
Extend the force arrow into a long line,

63
00:02:11,075 --> 00:02:12,241
called its line of action.

64
00:02:12,865 --> 00:02:14,898
Now find the shortest distance from the pivot

65
00:02:15,019 --> 00:02:15,643
to that line.

66
00:02:16,226 --> 00:02:18,255
It meets the line at right angles, and

67
00:02:18,295 --> 00:02:20,886
it's called the perpendicular distance, or the moment

68
00:02:20,967 --> 00:02:21,107
arm.

69
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:24,277
Here it's zero point two five times sin

70
00:02:24,317 --> 00:02:26,001
thirty, twelve and a half centimetres.

71
00:02:26,685 --> 00:02:29,094
Eighty newtons times zero point one two five

72
00:02:29,155 --> 00:02:31,202
metres is ten newton metres again.

73
00:02:31,986 --> 00:02:35,252
Either way, tau equals r F sin theta,

74
00:02:35,674 --> 00:02:38,077
which you'll also see written as r perpendicular

75
00:02:38,419 --> 00:02:38,859
times F.

76
00:02:39,689 --> 00:02:39,929
So

77
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:42,606
what about the push straight along the handle?

78
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:45,313
Its line of action passes right through the

79
00:02:45,334 --> 00:02:47,978
pivot, so the moment arm is zero, and

80
00:02:48,039 --> 00:02:48,780
so is the torque.

81
00:02:49,482 --> 00:02:52,206
In the formula, theta is the angle between

82
00:02:52,307 --> 00:02:52,907
two arrows:

83
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:55,151
one from the pivot out to where the

84
00:02:55,192 --> 00:02:55,832
force acts,

85
00:02:56,374 --> 00:02:57,355
and the force itself.

86
00:02:58,098 --> 00:03:00,868
Pushing towards the nut, those arrows point opposite

87
00:03:00,929 --> 00:03:03,016
ways, so theta is one hundred and eighty

88
00:03:03,057 --> 00:03:03,458
degrees.

89
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:06,078
Pulling outwards, theta is zero.

90
00:03:06,704 --> 00:03:08,098
The sine of either is zero.

91
00:03:08,664 --> 00:03:10,412
No turning, however hard you push.

92
00:03:11,076 --> 00:03:12,539
That's why a door won't open if you

93
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:14,525
push its edge straight towards the hinges.

94
00:03:15,068 --> 00:03:16,334
Put it together on a graph.

95
00:03:16,918 --> 00:03:18,627
Torque follows sin theta.

96
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:21,991
It's zero along the handle, grows as the

97
00:03:22,032 --> 00:03:25,193
force swings round, peaks at ninety degrees, and

98
00:03:25,274 --> 00:03:27,034
falls back to zero at one eighty.

99
00:03:27,739 --> 00:03:29,052
That's the answer to our question.

100
00:03:29,698 --> 00:03:31,280
Only the part of the force at right

101
00:03:31,361 --> 00:03:32,743
angles to the handle turns it,

102
00:03:33,285 --> 00:03:35,087
and that part is biggest when the whole

103
00:03:35,148 --> 00:03:36,490
force is at right angles.

104
00:03:37,235 --> 00:03:38,527
Torque also has a direction.

105
00:03:39,174 --> 00:03:41,103
This push turns the nut anticlockwise.

106
00:03:41,567 --> 00:03:42,372
Push the opposite way,

107
00:03:42,877 --> 00:03:43,864
and it turns it clockwise.

108
00:03:44,504 --> 00:03:45,617
Two slips to avoid.

109
00:03:46,262 --> 00:03:48,491
First, using the full handle length with an

110
00:03:48,711 --> 00:03:51,261
angled force, as if tau were always r

111
00:03:51,281 --> 00:03:51,321
F.

112
00:03:53,048 --> 00:03:55,461
For the thirty degree push, that gives twenty

113
00:03:55,742 --> 00:03:56,366
instead of ten.

114
00:03:57,292 --> 00:03:59,129
Second, using the wrong angle.

115
00:03:59,810 --> 00:04:01,532
If a question gives the angle between the

116
00:04:01,612 --> 00:04:03,873
force and a line at right angles to

117
00:04:03,913 --> 00:04:06,295
the handle, the sine of that angle is

118
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:07,236
the wrong factor.

119
00:04:08,118 --> 00:04:10,127
Always use the angle between the force and

120
00:04:10,168 --> 00:04:12,157
the line from the pivot to where it

121
00:04:12,299 --> 00:04:12,499
acts.

122
00:04:13,122 --> 00:04:14,150
Here's the short version.

123
00:04:14,714 --> 00:04:16,491
Push square, push far.

124
00:04:17,054 --> 00:04:19,417
Square means at right angles to the line

125
00:04:19,437 --> 00:04:19,977
from the pivot,

126
00:04:20,498 --> 00:04:22,801
and far means a long moment arm.

127
00:04:23,402 --> 00:04:25,033
Both make the turning effect bigger.

128
00:04:25,795 --> 00:04:27,697
And if the force's line of action passes

129
00:04:27,737 --> 00:04:28,338
through the pivot,

130
00:04:29,018 --> 00:04:30,020
no amount of pushing helps.

131
00:04:32,625 --> 00:04:33,074
Your turn.

132
00:04:34,102 --> 00:04:36,404
A force of forty newtons acts on the

133
00:04:36,624 --> 00:04:37,465
end of a lever,

134
00:04:37,986 --> 00:04:40,829
zero point three zero metres from its pivot.

135
00:04:41,731 --> 00:04:43,916
Calculate the torque about the pivot when the

136
00:04:43,956 --> 00:04:45,741
force is at right angles to the lever,

137
00:04:46,362 --> 00:04:48,166
and when it's at thirty degrees to the

138
00:04:48,207 --> 00:04:48,427
lever.

139
00:04:49,232 --> 00:04:50,057
Pause here to answer.

140
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:02,942
At right angles, tau equals r F,

141
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:06,585
zero point three zero times forty, twelve newton

142
00:05:06,605 --> 00:05:06,906
metres.

143
00:05:07,666 --> 00:05:10,549
At thirty degrees, multiply by sin thirty, a

144
00:05:10,589 --> 00:05:13,171
half, giving six point zero newton metres.

145
00:05:13,932 --> 00:05:16,667
The classic slip is using cos thirty, which

146
00:05:16,707 --> 00:05:17,973
gives about ten point four.

147
00:05:18,537 --> 00:05:20,489
The angle is between the force and the

148
00:05:20,591 --> 00:05:21,818
lever, so it's sine.

149
00:05:23,411 --> 00:05:23,858
Last one.

150
00:05:25,069 --> 00:05:27,530
A force of forty newtons acts on the

151
00:05:27,711 --> 00:05:30,272
end of a zero point three zero metre

152
00:05:30,313 --> 00:05:33,375
handle, directed along the handle towards the pivot.

153
00:05:34,217 --> 00:05:36,678
State the moment arm and the torque about

154
00:05:36,719 --> 00:05:37,079
the pivot,

155
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:39,581
and the angle between the force and the

156
00:05:39,642 --> 00:05:41,503
line from the pivot to the point where

157
00:05:41,524 --> 00:05:41,943
it acts.

158
00:05:42,768 --> 00:05:43,659
Pause here to answer.

159
00:05:54,515 --> 00:05:56,698
The line of action passes through the pivot,

160
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,483
so the moment arm is zero, and the

161
00:05:59,544 --> 00:06:01,587
torque is zero newton metres.

162
00:06:02,589 --> 00:06:04,590
The line from the pivot points out along

163
00:06:04,631 --> 00:06:06,892
the handle, and the force points back in,

164
00:06:07,313 --> 00:06:09,273
so the angle is one hundred and eighty

165
00:06:09,314 --> 00:06:09,754
degrees.

166
00:06:10,676 --> 00:06:12,763
Sin one eighty is zero, which agrees.

167
00:06:13,867 --> 00:06:15,528
The slip is writing r F,

168
00:06:16,149 --> 00:06:18,850
twelve newton metres, because the force is far

169
00:06:18,931 --> 00:06:19,531
from the pivot.

170
00:06:20,215 --> 00:06:22,250
Distance alone doesn't turn anything.

171
00:06:23,941 --> 00:06:27,028
So one force's torque depends on its size,

172
00:06:27,329 --> 00:06:29,695
its distance from the pivot, and its angle.

173
00:06:30,316 --> 00:06:33,099
But real objects usually have several forces turning

174
00:06:33,119 --> 00:06:36,321
them at once, some clockwise and some anticlockwise.

175
00:06:37,202 --> 00:06:39,325
A tower crane holds a heavy load far

176
00:06:39,405 --> 00:06:41,807
out on one side, and a counterweight close

177
00:06:41,847 --> 00:06:42,468
in on the other.

178
00:06:43,191 --> 00:06:44,858
How do those turning effects balance?

179
00:06:47,528 --> 00:06:48,350
Here are the key notes.

180
00:06:48,874 --> 00:06:50,421
Pause the video if you need more time

181
00:06:50,462 --> 00:06:50,883
to copy them.
